				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/292/545/392/399/405/original/dd521c9d2f72660c.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2023-11-16T11:47:16.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@sil\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>sil</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://platopunk.com/@khoi\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>khoi</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> This (for why I voted no on WebP).</p><p>I also have learned Can I Use is not guaranteed to be accurate and browser vendors (Safari) actively tweak support charts via PRs to make their support for a technology look better than it is. I no longer decide based on its charts.</p><p>Also also, lack of support outside browsers given the images I use sometimes get pulled into non-web contexts.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
